<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herzberg am Harz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herzberg am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> is a town on the south-western edge of the Harz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its hilltop castle served as a residence of the Guelph dukes of Grubenhagen until their line died out in 1596, after which it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and Hanoverian branches. The half-timbered castle is one of the largest of its kind in Lower Saxony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herzberg-am-harz.jpg" alt="Herzberg am Harz" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Binder · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzberg_Schlossblick.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Herzberg am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, on the south-western rim of the Harz mountain range and the Harz National Park. The town centre stands on the Sieber river, about 32 km north-east of Göttingen and 90 km south-east of the state capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. The municipal area also takes in the villages of Lonau, Pöhlde, Scharzfeld and Sieber. Natural monuments in the surrounding area include the Unicorn Cave, the Karst Trail and the Rhume Spring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Herzberg Castle, in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, was first mentioned in a deed of 1143. The Saxon Welf dynasty seized it in 1144, and in 1158 it became the property of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> with the consent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. It formed part of the Grubenhagen estates that the Welf duke Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> received in 1291, when he and his brothers divided their inheritance.</p><p>A settlement below the castle was first documented in 1337. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> rulers of the principality of Grubenhagen resided at the castle from 1486 until their line became extinct in 1596. In 1617 Duke George of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> had his inheritance rights confirmed by Emperor Matthias and lived there again with his wife Anne Eleonore of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, until in 1636 he moved his residence to the Leineschloss in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><p>Herzberg first developed as a centre of cloth and linen manufacturing. In the 18th and 19th centuries its important industries included brewing, metal founding and turning, agricultural machinery and boot making, as well as arms production for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> forces. It received town privileges in 1929, when it was part of the Prussian Province of Hanover.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Herzberg is twinned with Góra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, a link dating from 1993. In 2006 the city council decided to present the town as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esperanto">Esperanto</a> city, Esperanto-urbo, called Hercbergo-ĉe-Harco in Esperanto, and it is the home of the Interkultura Centro Herzberg, a centre promoting the international auxiliary language Esperanto. Local employers include a paper and corrugated cardboard mill, a foundry and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and publishing house. The social welfare pioneer Ida Arenhold (1798 to 1863) and the Prussian colonel general Karl von Einem (1853 to 1934) are among its notable people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herzberg am Harz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the sons of Albert I of Brunswick divided their inheritance around 1291, Henry I the Admirable received Grubenhagen, named after a castle near Einbeck, with lands in the western Harz around Osterode and <b>Herzberg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-grubenhagen">Principality of Grubenhagen</a></li></ul></section>
